---
// Terms of service for the hosted tier (tracker#10).
//
// Deliberately short. The hosted service is free, in beta, run by one person,
// and every user has a no-permission-needed exit because the software is AGPL
// and self-hostable — so the honest document is a description of that
// arrangement, not an enterprise SaaS agreement.
//
// NOT REVIEWED BY COUNSEL, and there is no governing-law clause: the legal
// entity and its jurisdiction are undecided (tracker#10), and inventing one
// would be worse than naming the gap.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { APP_URL, LAST_UPDATED, PRIVACY_PAGE_URL, SELFHOST_URL } from '../config';
---

<Base
  title="Terms"
  description="The terms for the free hosted HomeAccounting service: what we provide, what we ask of you, and how either of us can walk away."
>
  <section class="container max-w-3xl py-20">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold">Terms of service</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted-foreground">
      These cover the hosted service at <strong>homeaccounting.com</strong>. The
      software itself is licensed separately, under the AGPL-3.0 — if you <a
        class="underline"
        href={SELFHOST_URL}>run it yourself</a
      >, the licence is your agreement and this page is not.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Last updated {LAST_UPDATED}.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">1. Who you are agreeing with</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      The service is operated by the maintainer of HomeAccounting as an
      individual. There is no company behind it yet. When one is formed, it will
      take over this agreement and we will say so on this page.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">2. What you get</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      A hosted instance of HomeAccounting, free of charge, in beta. There is no
      uptime commitment, no support commitment, and no guarantee that a feature
      that works today works tomorrow. We do not take payment, so there is
      nothing to refund and no billing data to lose.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      HomeAccounting is a bookkeeping tool. It is not a bank, not a payment
      service, and nothing it shows you is financial, tax or investment advice.
      Figures it derives from your records — balances, reports, categorisations
      — can be wrong, and you are the one who has to check them before relying
      on them.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">3. Your account</h2>
    <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        Keep your password to yourself. Anything done with your credentials is
        treated as done by you.
      </li>
      <li>
        Give us an email address that reaches you — it is the only way we can
        warn you about a security problem.
      </li>
      <li>You need to be old enough to enter a contract where you live.</li>
      <li>
        If you connect a bank, you are confirming you are entitled to access
        that account. We act on your instruction; we have no relationship with
        your bank.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">4. What we ask you not to do</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      Do not attack the service, try to reach other people&rsquo;s data, upload
      malware, or use it to break the law. Do not hammer the API hard enough to
      degrade it for everyone else — it is one small server. Security research
      is welcome and explicitly not a breach of these terms, provided you follow
      the <a class="underline" href="/security">disclosure policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">5. Your data stays yours</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      We claim no ownership of anything you put in. We use it to run the service
      for you and for nothing else. What we hold, where it lives, and who else
      it touches are set out in the <a class="underline" href={PRIVACY_PAGE_URL}
        >privacy notice</a
      >, which is part of these terms.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">6. Ending it</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      You can stop using the service whenever you like and ask us to delete your
      account. We can suspend or close an account that is attacking the service
      or being used illegally; short of that, we will not close your account
      without telling you why.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      We may also discontinue the hosted service. If we do, we will give at
      least 30 days&rsquo; notice and help you get your data out. This is the
      part the open-source licence is load-bearing for: the same stack that runs
      homeaccounting.com is published, so shutting down the hosted tier cannot
      strand you — you can stand it up yourself.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">7. No warranty, limited liability</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      The service is provided as-is, without warranties of any kind, to the
      extent the law where you live allows. We are not liable for indirect or
      consequential loss, or for loss of data or profit. Nothing here limits
      liability that cannot legally be limited — including for death, personal
      injury, fraud, or your rights as a consumer, which these terms do not take
      away.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      Keep your own backups of anything you cannot afford to lose. That is
      sensible advice for any free beta service, this one included.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-2xl font-bold">8. Changes</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
      This page lives in the public site repository, so every change to it has a
      diff and a date. For a change that materially affects you we will give
      notice before it takes effect; continuing to use the service afterwards is
      how you accept it.
    </p>

    <p class="mt-14 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Neither these terms nor the <a class="underline" href={PRIVACY_PAGE_URL}
        >privacy notice</a
      > has been reviewed by a lawyer, and neither names a governing law, because
      the project has no legal entity yet. They describe the arrangement honestly
      in the meantime. Questions: <a
        class="underline"
        href="mailto:legal@homeaccounting.com">legal@homeaccounting.com</a
      >. Ready to start? <a class="underline" href={APP_URL}>Sign up</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
